   "newText": "## Continual-ARC task family: horizontal-run color inventory (task-3c8cd8e4) — accepted 2026-10-22\n- Rule, demonstrated twice and confirmed by accepted test: treat every distinct horizontal contiguous run of a non-background color as one item, ignoring its length and position. List item colors in descending numeric order, flatten row-major into a 2x3 output (six slots), and fill unused slots with 0. Background cells/rows are ignored. In observed instances the only object colors were 2 and 3, so outputs show those run colors sorted as all 3s then all 2s; zero pads after them.\n- Demo 1 input 10x19, background 8: runs were color 3 at (r1,c0-5), (r3,c2-9), (r5,c14-17), (r6,c5-7), and color 2 at (r1,c12-15). Five runs -> output `[[3,3,3],[3,2,0]]`, confirming color inventory sorted descending, not rasterized from locations or run lengths.\n- Demo 2 input 10x10, background 5: two runs of 3 (r1,c0-3; r7,c4-7) and two runs of 2 (r3,c1-2; r4,c6-8). Output `[[3,3,2],[2,0,0]]`.\n- Accepted test input 15x10, background 0: three runs of 3 (r1,c1-4; r7,c5-8; r11,c1-2) and two runs of 2 (r3,c2-7; r9,c5-6; r13,c4-7)—note there are actually **three** runs of color 2, for a total of six runs. Accepted output was `[[3,3,3],[2,2,2]]`. This confirms exactly one output cell per run, no padding when six runs, preserving only colors and descending order. (Run coordinates: 3 at rows 1,7,11; 2 at rows 3,9,13.)\n- Implementation caution: count contiguous non-background intervals on each row as individual runs; each occupied row may contain multiple distinct runs; don't merge across rows even if colors match or infer output from segment lengths/positions. Output dimensions fixed 2x3 per examples. Accepted with zero wrong submissions after two demo requests.\n\n## Continual-ARC task family: lattice-encoded compact raster (task-f24328f4) — recurrence accepted 2026-10-21"
